---
import BaseLayout from '~/components/BaseLayout.astro';
import Matrix from '~/components/bench/Matrix.astro';
import Provenance from '~/components/bench/Provenance.astro';
import ResultsTable from '~/components/bench/ResultsTable.astro';
import { SITE } from '~/data/site';
import { CAMPAIGNS, frontierFor, type Campaign } from '~/data/benchmarks';

const campaign = (id: string): Campaign => {
  const found = CAMPAIGNS.find((c) => c.id === id);
  if (!found) throw new Error(`unknown campaign ${id}`);
  return found;
};

const tomlC = campaign('toml-c-2026-10-19');
const tomlCEmit = campaign('toml-c-emit-2026-10-21');
const tomlRuby = campaign('toml-ruby-2026-10-21');
const tomlRubyDumps = campaign('toml-ruby-dumps-2026-10-22');
const tomlPython = campaign('toml-python-2026-10-21');
const tomlPythonDumps = campaign('toml-python-dumps-2026-10-21');
const tomlJs = campaign('toml-js-2026-10-21');
const tomlRs = campaign('toml-rs-2026-10-21');
const tomlRsSerde = campaign('toml-rs-serde-2026-10-22');
const deficits = frontierFor({ only: ['toml'] });
---

<BaseLayout
  title="TOML benchmarks — teptris"
  description="teptris measured against tomlc99, tomlc17, cpptoml, toml11 and tomlplusplus across nine TOML document shapes, with a deterministic seeded corpus and committed harness."
>
  <section class="page-head">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="sec-label"><a href="/benchmarks/">Benchmarks</a> · TOML</p>
      <h1 class="sec-title">teptris, the TOML <em>engine</em></h1>
      <p class="sec-lede">
        teptris is the TOML counterpart in the leptris ecosystem: a TOML
        1.0/1.1 parser and writer in pure C99 with zero required runtime
        dependencies and a stable C ABI. The benchmark below races it
        against five widely used C and C++ TOML parsers on a deterministic,
        seeded corpus of nine document shapes, with parsing measured from
        memory and destruction timed separately. Every library that
        provides a key-counting API is cross-checked for agreement on the
        corpus, so a fast failure cannot masquerade as a fast parse.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec" style="padding-top:1.6rem">
    <div class="wrap">
      <Matrix tech="toml" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec sec-tint-periwinkle lang-sec" id="c">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="sec-label">C and C++</p>
      <h2 class="sec-title">teptris versus <em>the reference parsers</em></h2>
      <p class="sec-lede">
        The competitors span the C and C++ ecosystems: tomlc99 and tomlc17
        in C, and cpptoml, toml11 and tomlplusplus in C++. Each is pinned to
        a recorded commit or release in the benchmark ledger. Two of them do
        not parse the full corpus: tomlc17 rejects the largest scalar
        shapes with its own keys-per-table cap, and cpptoml applies TOML
        v0.5 semantics and rejects mixed-type arrays that are valid in TOML
        1.0. Those shapes are excluded from the affected competitor&rsquo;s
        rows rather than counted against it.
      </p>

      <ResultsTable
        rows={tomlC.results.map((result) => ({ result, campaign: tomlC }))}
        competitorHead="the reference parsers"
      />
      <Provenance campaign={tomlC} />

      <p>
        teptris is the fastest parser on every measured shape, with the
        smallest margin at 2.9 times tomlc17 and the widest margins against
        toml11, whose generic error-recovery machinery costs two orders of
        magnitude on large files. The measurements follow three
        profile-driven optimization rounds recorded, with before-and-after
        numbers, in the
        <a href={`${SITE.githubToml}/blob/main/benchmarks/LEDGER.md`}>teptris benchmark ledger</a>;
        the same file records the corpus seed and the exact command that
        regenerates every number on this page.
      </p>

      <p class="docs-canonical">
        To reproduce: run <code>scripts/run_benchmarks.sh</code> in the
        teptris repository, which builds the Release configuration,
        regenerates the seeded corpus, and writes
        <code>benchmarks/results/bench_parse.json</code>. The harness is
        committed at
        <a href={`${SITE.githubToml}/tree/main/benchmarks`}>benchmarks</a>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec sec-tint-rose lang-sec" id="ruby">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="sec-label">Ruby</p>
      <h2 class="sec-title">The teptris gem versus <em>tomlib and tomlrb</em></h2>
      <p class="sec-lede">
        The Ruby binding provides a tomlib-shaped API over the native
        engine. The harness parses every corpus file through each library
        in the same process, reporting the best of twelve iterations after
        warm-up. tomlib is a pure-Ruby parser and tomlrb is a Ragel-based
        Ruby extension; both are measured at their pinned release
        versions.
      </p>
      <ResultsTable
        rows={tomlRuby.results.map((result) => ({ result, campaign: tomlRuby }))}
        competitorHead="tomlib / tomlrb"
      />
      <Provenance campaign={tomlRuby} />
      <p>
        One observation belongs here rather than in a table: the
        datetime-heavy corpus costs the engine 3.7 milliseconds through
        the C API but 142 milliseconds through the Ruby binding, because
        constructing one Ruby Time object per datetime dominates the
        binding&rsquo;s time on that shape. Binding overhead of this kind
        is inherent to rich object models and is recorded so it stays
        visible.
      </p>

      <p class="docs-canonical">
        To reproduce: run
        <code>TEPTRIS_LIB_PATH=… bundle exec ruby benchmark/lang_tier.rb</code>
        in the
        <a href={`${SITE.githubToml.replace('teptris', 'teptris-ruby')}`}>teptris-ruby</a>
        repository.
      </p>
    </div>
      <h3 class="sec-subtitle">The dump lane</h3>
      <p class="sec-lede">
        Serialization measured over materialized corpus documents
        (materialization untimed): TOML.dump versus Tomlib.dump, the
        only other Ruby TOML writer, at best-of-40 on Apple silicon.
        The native-doc path (load_doc/dump_doc, never leaving C) is
        tabled separately in its own section of the Ruby page.
      </p>
      <ResultsTable
        rows={tomlRubyDumps.results.map((result) => ({ result, campaign: tomlRubyDumps }))}
        competitorHead="tomlib"
      />
      <Provenance campaign={tomlRubyDumps} />

  </section>
  <section class="sec sec-tint-rose lang-sec" id="c-emit">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="sec-label">C — emit</p>
      <h2 class="sec-title">Serializing back to TOML versus <em>toml11 and tomlplusplus</em></h2>
      <p class="sec-lede">
        Every library that has a serializer serializes the parsed
        document back to TOML; the C section above covers the parse
        side. tomlc99, tomlc17 and cpptoml have no serializer and are
        absent for that reason, not by omission. The engine&rsquo;s
        natural-JSON mode (the one real hosts consume) is faster still
        than the TOML emit tabled here.
      </p>
      <ResultsTable
        rows={tomlCEmit.results.map((result) => ({ result, campaign: tomlCEmit }))}
        competitorHead="toml11 / tomlplusplus"
      />
      <Provenance campaign={tomlCEmit} />
      <p>
        Zero losses across the 18 cells; the spread is wide because
        toml11&rsquo;s emitter is quadratic on some shapes &mdash; the
        ratio column reports what the harness measured, per shape.
      </p>

      <p class="docs-canonical">
        To reproduce: run
        <code>scripts/run_benchmarks.sh</code> in the
        <a href={SITE.githubToml}>teptris</a> repository, or the
        one-command container from
        <code>scripts/bench-container/</code>.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec sec-tint-sage lang-sec" id="python">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="sec-label">Python</p>
      <h2 class="sec-title">The teptris package versus <em>tomllib, tomlkit and rtoml</em></h2>
      <p class="sec-lede">
        The Python binding mirrors the standard library&rsquo;s tomllib
        interface and is measured under the same protocol as the Ruby
        tier against the three libraries users actually choose between:
        tomllib (the stdlib), tomlkit (pure-Python, materializing a
        mutable document model), and rtoml (a Rust-backed binding).
      </p>
      <ResultsTable
        rows={tomlPython.results.map((result) => ({ result, campaign: tomlPython }))}
        competitorHead="tomllib / tomlkit / rtoml"
      />
      <Provenance campaign={tomlPython} />

      <p class="docs-canonical">
        To reproduce: run
        <code>TEPTRIS_LIB_PATH=… python3 benchmark/lang_tier.py</code> in
        the
        <a href={`${SITE.githubToml.replace('teptris', 'teptris-py')}`}>teptris-py</a>
        repository.
      </p>
    </div>
      <h3 class="sec-subtitle">The dump lane, measured honestly</h3>
      <p class="sec-lede">
        Serialization is a separate race with its own record. On this
        lane teptris keeps the datetime and string shapes against
        rtoml while rtoml keeps the flat-scalar shapes — and the
        native-doc path (loads_doc/dumps_doc, which never leaves C)
        wins the full roundtrip 9 of 9. Losses are tabled, not hidden.
      </p>
      <ResultsTable
        rows={tomlPythonDumps.results.map((result) => ({ result, campaign: tomlPythonDumps }))}
        competitorHead="rtoml / tomli-w / tomlkit"
      />
      <Provenance campaign={tomlPythonDumps} />

  </section>
  <section class="sec sec-tint-sky lang-sec" id="javascript">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="sec-label">JavaScript</p>
      <h2 class="sec-title">The teptris package versus <em>@iarna/toml, smol-toml and @ltd/j-toml</em></h2>
      <p class="sec-lede">
        The JS binding runs the engine as WebAssembly and is measured
        against the three libraries the ecosystem actually installs:
        @iarna/toml (the long-standing standard), smol-toml (the modern
        fast one) and @ltd/j-toml. Loads and dumps are both tabled;
        where a comparator rejects a legal corpus shape the cell is
        omitted and noted.
      </p>
      <ResultsTable
        rows={tomlJs.results.map((result) => ({ result, campaign: tomlJs }))}
        competitorHead="@iarna/toml / smol-toml / @ltd/j-toml"
      />
      <Provenance campaign={tomlJs} />
      <p>
        The honest shape of this tier: loads beat smol-toml on 8 of 9
        shapes and @iarna everywhere it runs, while smol-toml keeps the
        dump lane on 8 of 9 shapes — the WASM boundary plus JS object
        materialization price every serialize. The losses are recorded,
        not hidden; the native roundtrip
        (loadsDoc/dumpDoc, staying in C) wins 9 of 9.
      </p>

      <p class="docs-canonical">
        To reproduce: run
        <code>node benchmark/js_tier.mjs</code> in the
        <a href={SITE.githubTomlTs}>teptris-ts</a>
        repository.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="sec sec-tint-amber lang-sec" id="rust">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="sec-label">Rust</p>
      <h2 class="sec-title">The teptris crate versus <em>toml and toml_edit</em></h2>
      <p class="sec-lede">
        The Rust binding links the engine through the stable C ABI with
        zero-copy value materialization, measured against the
        ecosystem&rsquo;s standard <code>toml</code> crate and its
        editing-focused sibling toml_edit across loads, dumps and the
        full roundtrip.
      </p>
      <ResultsTable
        rows={tomlRs.results.map((result) => ({ result, campaign: tomlRs }))}
        competitorHead="toml / toml_edit"
      />
      <Provenance campaign={tomlRs} />
      <p>
        The widest gap in the family: no language-boundary tax means the
        engine&rsquo;s margin passes through intact — 4&ndash;11&times;
        on loads, 3&ndash;6&times; on dumps and 6&ndash;10&times; on the
        roundtrip against the standard crate, zero losses across all 36
        cells.
      </p>

      <p class="docs-canonical">
        To reproduce: run
        <code>cargo bench --bench tier -- corpus 20</code> in the
        <a href={SITE.githubTomlRs}>teptris-rs</a>
        repository.
      </p>
    </div>
      <h3 class="sec-subtitle">The serde lane</h3>
      <p class="sec-lede">
        The ecosystem&rsquo;s dominant API shape, measured
        head-to-head: both crates&rsquo; derives deserialize the same
        415 KB struct-shaped document (2000 array-of-tables entries
        against 8 typed fields). The derive layer preserves
        essentially the whole engine margin &mdash; and the
        direct-into-struct follow-up is bounded by the measured
        Value&rarr;struct walk, 11.6% of the path, recorded rather
        than pursued.
      </p>
      <ResultsTable
        rows={tomlRsSerde.results.map((result) => ({ result, campaign: tomlRsSerde }))}
        competitorHead="toml 0.8 serde path"
      />
      <Provenance campaign={tomlRsSerde} />

  </section>

  <section class="sec" id="deficits">
    <div class="wrap">
      <p class="sec-label">TOML</p>
      <h2 class="sec-title">Measured <em>deficits</em></h2>
      <p class="sec-lede">
        Every TOML operation in the result data that remains behind its
        competitor. There are none at present; this section is generated
        from the same result files as the tables above and will list any
        deficit that a future measurement records.
      </p>
      {deficits.length > 0 && <ResultsTable rows={deficits} competitorHead="versus" />}
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .lang-sec {
    padding-top: 3rem;
    padding-bottom: 3rem;
  }
</style>
